> @sonukapoor, since you wrote the PR that brought this regression, would you have any particular opinions about this issue ? were those use-cases considered in your PR ?

@PowerKiKi I do not think this use case of null/undefined was considered. 
@iRealNirmal let us know if you need any help with that.

As discussed offline the `min` attribute is being removed when the value is 0 is because of this `host` binding:

```ts
@Directive({
  selector:
      'input[type=number][min][formControlName],input[type=number][min][formControl],input[type=number][min][ngModel]',
  providers: [MIN_VALIDATOR],
  host: {'[attr.min]': 'min ? min : null'}
})
```

review_comments:
I believe the tests added in this PR would pass even with the changes in the `packages/forms/src/directives/validators.ts` file. The original problem is that the `min` and `max` attributes are not set on the underlying `<input>` element (via host bindings) when the min/max validator directives are invoked. In addition to the current assertions you should add some checks that `min` and/or `max` attributes are set on an input. You can inspect the contents of the `fixture.nativeElement` DOM element to see how it changes with and without the fix. So one more condition that you can add (to make the test fail without the fix and work after the fix) may look like this:

```
// note: the `input` is declared as `const` above, so the script would fail on this line and we'd need to change `const` -> `let` above.
let input = fixture.debugElement.query(By.css('input')).nativeElement;
expect(input.getAttribute('min')).toEqual('0');
expect(input.getAttribute('max')).toEqual('0');
```
